---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<BaseLayout title="Online resources">
  <span class="eyebrow">Research toolkit</span>
  <h1>Online resources</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Public resources on the web that can move the <a href={withBase('/research/')}>open
    questions</a> forward, grouped by cost. The two most decisive record sets — Larry's
    personnel file and unit morning reports — are <em>not</em> online anywhere; those come only
    by written request to the National Personnel Records Center, and are tracked in the
    project's research issues.
  </p>

  <h2>Free</h2>
  <ul class="resource-list">
    <li>
      <p class="record">Digital archive &middot; Texas Tech University</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.vietnam.ttu.edu/virtualarchive/">Virtual Vietnam Archive</a></h3>
      <p>
        Roughly seven million pages of digitized documents, free to search and download: unit
        records, after-action reports, period military maps, photographs, and oral histories.
        The richest single place to look for candidate units in the
        <a href={withBase('/units/')}>118th question</a>, for Nha Trang and
        Núi Bà Đen in 1964&ndash;65, for period maps bearing on
        <a href={withBase('/places/thom-son-nuht/')}>&ldquo;Thom Son Nuht,&rdquo;</a> and for
        names like <a href={withBase('/people/captain-guegler/')}>Captain Guegler</a> in
        digitized paperwork.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Casualty memorial &middot; unit-indexed</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.virtualwall.org/">The Virtual Wall</a></h3>
      <p>
        Memorial pages for every name on the Vietnam Veterans Memorial, browsable by unit of
        assignment (unit data derived from the volunteer-researched Coffelt Database). Two uses here:
        searching spelling variants of <a href={withBase('/people/sgt-lowry/')}>Sgt.
        Lowry</a> and <a href={withBase('/people/captain-guegler/')}>Captain Guegler</a>, and
        browsing Special Forces losses in the Núi Bà Đen / Tây Ninh area. A miss is
        information too — it suggests the man survived the war, pointing research toward
        association rosters instead.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Casualty memorial &middot; name search &middot; outreach</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.vvmf.org/Wall-of-Faces/">Wall of Faces</a></h3>
      <p>
        The Vietnam Veterans Memorial Fund's photo-and-remembrance page for each of the 58,000+
        names. Complements the Virtual Wall for name searches, and posted remembrances
        sometimes reach family members and unit-mates — a quiet outreach channel.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veterans association &middot; unit history &middot; archived</p>
      <h3><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20191226004046/http://www.118ahc.org/">118th Assault Helicopter Company association (archived)</a></h3>
      <p>
        The &ldquo;Thunderbirds&rdquo; association site is gone — its old domain has been taken
        over by an unrelated spam operator, so only the Internet Archive copy above should be
        used. The archived site preserves unit history, member stories, and photographs, and a
        companion history page at the <a href="https://www.145thcab.com/unit-htms/118ahc.htm">145th
        Combat Aviation Battalion site</a> is still live. Directly relevant to whether Larry's
        remembered &ldquo;118th AOD&rdquo; connects to the 118th Aviation Company
        (Bien Hoa, 1963&ndash;65); for reaching men who served, the 145th CAB site and the VHPA
        below are the live channels.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veterans association &middot; outreach</p>
      <h3><a href="https://tsna.org/">Tan Son Nhut Association</a></h3>
      <p>
        Association of veterans who served at Tân Sơn Nhứt — predominantly Air Force, though
        the base hosted Army personnel too — with guestbooks and bulletin boards. If &ldquo;<a href={withBase('/places/thom-son-nuht/')}>Thom Son Nuht</a>&rdquo;
        is a phonetic memory of Tân Sơn Nhứt — the leading hypothesis — this is both a way to
        test it and a channel for finding men who were there in 1964&ndash;65.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Official histories &middot; free PDFs</p>
      <h3><a href="https://history.army.mil/">U.S. Army Center of Military History</a></h3>
      <p>
        Free official histories of the war, including the airmobility volumes that trace the
        aviation build-up — the 14th Aviation Battalion's move to Nha Trang in October
        1964 and the 1st Cavalry Division's advance party staging through Nha Trang in August
        1965, both of which bear on <a href={withBase('/units/')}>the unit question</a>. The Air Force and
        Marine Corps history offices likewise publish free PDFs of the volumes cited on the
        <a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Sources</a> page.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Government archive catalog</p>
      <h3><a href="https://catalog.archives.gov/">National Archives Catalog</a></h3>
      <p>
        Finding aids and a growing body of digitized series, including Record Group 472
        (Records of the U.S. Forces in Southeast Asia, 1950&ndash;1975 — MACV and all
        services). The place to scope what exists before requesting unit records — and
        to learn box-level citations a researcher at College Park would pull.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Veterans association &middot; partially public</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.vhpa.org/">Vietnam Helicopter Pilots Association</a></h3>
      <p>
        A pilots' association spanning the Vietnam era, 1961&ndash;75, whose public value here
        is its photo collection and helicopter-incident pages; the member directory itself is
        member-oriented. Context for Larry's helicopter-travel recollections and another
        outreach avenue.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Paid</h2>
  <ul class="resource-list">
    <li>
      <p class="record">Newspaper archive &middot; day pass $4.95, monthly $7.95 (rates as of 2026)</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.stripes.com/help/archives-faq/">Stars and Stripes historic archives</a></h3>
      <p>
        Every Pacific edition 1948&ndash;1999, digitized and keyword-searchable via
        NewspaperArchive. The direct route to the erroneous killed-in-action report: search the
        surname and near-misspellings across Pacific editions of roughly July&ndash;September
        1965. A single day pass may be enough for a first pass.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Genealogy subscription</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/1139">Ancestry</a></h3>
      <p>
        Holds <em>Stars and Stripes</em> Pacific editions only through 1963 — too early for the
        KIA report — plus general military collections. Worth a search mainly if the family
        already has a subscription.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="record">Military records subscription</p>
      <h3><a href="https://www.fold3.com/">Fold3</a></h3>
      <p>
        Ancestry's military-records site, digitizing selected National Archives series.
        Vietnam-era coverage is thinner than for the World Wars; check the free index for
        relevant hits before paying.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not online</h2>
  <p>
    Larry's Official Military Personnel File and DD-214 must be requested from the National
    Personnel Records Center with his signature, and company morning reports — the top of this
    project's <a href={withBase('/research/')}>source priorities</a> — exist only on microfilm
    at the same records center. Both requests are tracked as research issues in the
    repository. Everything on this page is a way to make progress while that mail is in
    flight, and to know exactly what to ask for when it arrives.
  </p>

  <p><a href={withBase('/research/')}>&larr; Research</a></p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .resource-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .resource-list li {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 1.2rem 0;
  }

  .resource-list h3,
  .resource-list p {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  }
</style>
